---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import RouteParamInfo from '@/components/RouteParamInfo.astro';
---
<Layout title="Astro Dynamic Route Param Mapping: Best Practice">
  <main class="prose mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1>Astro Dynamic Route Param Mapping: Robust Best Practice</h1>
    <p><strong>Why is this important?</strong> Astro requires the param name in <code>params</code> (from <code>getStaticPaths</code>) to match the filename of your dynamic route. If they don’t match, your build will fail.</p>
    <h2>Centralized Mapping</h2>
    <p>To keep your code DRY and robust, use a single mapping file for all dynamic route param names. This ensures your static path generation and route files always stay in sync.</p>
    <pre><code>{`// src/utils/routeParamMap.js\n\nexport const routeParamMap = {\n  blog: 'blogslug',      // for src/pages/blog/[...blogslug].astro\n  projects: 'slug',      // for src/pages/projects/[...slug].astro\n  // Add more as needed\n};`}</code></pre>
    <h2>How to Use</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Import <code>routeParamMap</code> in your static path generator.</li>
      <li>Always use the mapped param name for <code>params</code> in <code>getStaticPaths</code>.</li>
      <li>Make sure your dynamic route filename matches the param name.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>Reusable Component</h2>
    <p>Use the <code>RouteParamInfo</code> component below to display this mapping anywhere in your docs or contributor pages.</p>
    <RouteParamInfo />
    <h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
    <p><strong>Never change a dynamic route filename or param name without updating the mapping in <code>routeParamMap.js</code>!</strong></p>
  </main>
</Layout>
